<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> What is the difference between a word's denotation and its connotation?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Denotation is its literal meaning; connotation is the associations and emotional shadings that come with it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Denotation is its sound; connotation is its spelling.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">They are two names for the same thing.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Denotation is its history; connotation is its length.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A poem's power often comes from the gap between what its words say literally and what they suggest.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Denotation is the literal, dictionary meaning. Connotation is everything the word suggests beyond that.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Why does the lesson say literature unfolds in time?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">You receive it one word at a time, holding earlier words in memory as new ones arrive.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Because every story must be set in a historical period.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Because poems are always about the past.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">Because books take years to print.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Unlike a painting, which gives you everything at once, a poem or story is sequential, and that sequence is part of its form.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Contrast literature with painting. A painting is all at once; a poem or story arrives word by word.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> Which kind of narrative integrates every detail so tightly that nothing can be removed without damaging the whole?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">A lyric.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">A quest narrative.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">An organic narrative.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">An episodic narrative.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Borges's story is the lesson's example: every detail connects to its central theme.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Episodic narratives are loosely connected adventures, and quest narratives follow a search. The one in which every detail depends on the others is organic.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> A quest narrative is built around</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">a series of loosely connected adventures with no central plot.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">a protagonist searching for something of supreme value.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">a single concentrated feeling about an object.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">a debate between two characters.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A quest narrative builds suspense around whether the protagonist will find what they seek.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The word quest names a search. The lesson describes it as a protagonist looking for something of supreme value.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> What does a lyric poem usually do?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">It reveals a single, concentrated feeling about something and captures a moment, mood, or insight.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">It explains a scientific theory.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">It lists historical dates.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">It tells a long story with many characters.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Unlike narrative, a lyric does not tell a story. Dickinson's and Hughes's poems are lyrics.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">A lyric is not mainly a story. It concentrates a feeling or insight into a short space.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> What is an objective correlative?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">A summary of a poem's plot.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">A comparison that uses the word like or as.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">An object or situation that embodies an emotion without naming it directly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">A rhyme at the end of a line.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A bare tree in winter, for example, can correlate with loss or emptiness without the poem saying so.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The key idea is expressing an emotion through an object or situation instead of naming it. That is the objective correlative.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> Which statement correctly distinguishes simile from metaphor?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">They are the same device.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">A simile makes a comparison explicit using like or as; a metaphor makes the comparison directly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">A simile is always longer than a metaphor.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">A metaphor uses like or as; a simile never does.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. "Like a raisin in the sun" is a simile; calling death a gentleman caller is a metaphor.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The words like and as mark a simile. A metaphor states the comparison directly.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> In Dickinson's "Because I could not stop for Death," what do the school, the gazing grain, and the setting sun represent as the carriage passes them?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Three symbols of the poet's religion.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Stages of life: childhood, maturity, and decline.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Three places the speaker visited on vacation.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Nothing; they are only scenery.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The poem never explains the images; it trusts them to carry the meaning, which is part of what makes it powerful.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The carriage passes images that stand for phases of a life. Think of the order: children at school, ripe grain, the end of the day.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> How does the last line of Hughes's "Harlem" differ from the rest of the poem?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">It describes the weather.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">It repeats the first line.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">It adds a sixth simile.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">It drops the similes and asks a single, direct question: Or does it explode?</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The shift from specific images to one abstract verb, set apart in italics, gives the ending enormous force.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The poem answers its opening question with five similes and then changes strategy. Look for what the final line stops doing.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> According to the Gallery lesson, why do most of Shakespeare's plays survive?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Shakespeare published them all himself while he was alive.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Only the theater companies remembered them.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">They were carved into stone.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">They were collected in a printed volume, the First Folio of 1623, seven years after his death.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. About half of his plays were first printed in the Folio. Literature depends on the physical form that carries it.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The Gallery highlights how books preserve literature. Shakespeare wrote for the stage; the collected volume that saved the plays appeared after his death.</div>
  </div>
</div>

